\label{sec:f2}

\looseness=-1 The factorial design isolates trained $\dt$-embedding as the strongest predictor. The \emph{primary} comparison, 008d (trained, subsample ON) vs.\ 008f (no embedding, subsample ON), differs only in embedding training, yielding $\nve = 4.12\times$ (eval 82.8) vs.\ $\nve \leq 1.69\times$ (eval 139.5--468.8$^f$). \emph{Secondary} comparisons against 008a/008c (unexposed embedding) co-vary subsample status and horizon alongside the embedding factor. The $\nve \geq 2.0\times$ threshold (35K+ window) classifies all $n{=}36$ configurations across two environments (9 CIVO-positive, 27 non-CIVO; AUROC${=}1.000$, F1${=}1.000$; Table~\ref{tab:nve_loeo}; Figure~\ref{fig:nve_trajectory}).

\looseness=-1 The trained $\dt$-embedding triggers CIVO onset (NVE $\geq 2.0\times$) in 3 of 5 Walker Walk seeds, degrading all five to below 30\% of baseline (interquartile mean [IQM] $148.8$, $n{=}5$; \citet{agarwal2021precipice}). Including Cheetah Run (\S\ref{sec:f6}), CIVO onset occurs in 5/7 multi-joint locomotion runs; 3 sustain collapse, one self-corrects, one shows NVE self-correction without policy recovery (Appendix~\ref{sec:seed_divergence}).

\paragraph{Mechanism evidence.}
\looseness=-1 The RSSM reward head develops a $1.63\times$ prediction gap between $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$ on Walker (Table~\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}), but Cheetah shows no reward gap ($1.00\times$) despite higher CIVO severity ($8.27\times$); reward fidelity degradation is one transmission pathway but not necessary. The critic internalizes the bias: mean $V(s)/\text{MC-return}$ reaches $7.80\times$ (100\% overestimated) vs.\ $1.05\times$ for the control. Evaluation return collapses ${\sim}35$K steps before actor entropy rises (Appendix~\ref{sec:civo_mechanism}).

